---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import { Link } from "../../../registry/ui/link";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";
import { applyFontsExample } from "../../data/snippets";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Installation | Buril UI"
  description="Install Buril components with the shadcn CLI, from a URL or by name."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Installation</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    Buril components install with the shadcn CLI, one component at a time, from a URL or by name.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Buril is a <dfn>registry</dfn>: a set of JSON files that the shadcn CLI reads. Each file
    describes one <dfn>item</dfn>, such as a component or the theme, with the source files and
    dependencies it installs. A <dfn>namespace</dfn>, such as <code>@buril</code>, is a short name
    for the registry, so you can install items by name. A <dfn>token</dfn> is a CSS variable, such
    as <code>--primary</code>, that components read for their colors and motion.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Requirements</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    A React 19 project with Tailwind CSS v4 that is set up for shadcn, which means it has a
    <code>components.json</code> file. If it does not, run the CLI's <code>init</code> first:
  </p>
  <InstallCommand id="install-init" commands={["init"]} />

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Install from a URL</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">Every component has its own JSON file in the registry. Pass its URL to add:</p>
  <InstallCommand id="install-url" commands={["add https://example.com/r/button.json"]} />

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Install by name</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Register the <code>@buril</code> namespace once. The CLI saves it in
    <code>components.json</code>, and from then on every component installs by name:
  </p>
  <InstallCommand
    id="install-namespace"
    commands={["registry add @buril=https://example.com/r/{name}.json", "add @buril/button"]}
  />

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">What the CLI adds</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      The component file in <code>components/ui/</code>, at the same path shadcn uses. If a file is
      already there, the CLI asks before replacing it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Its npm dependencies, such as <code>class-variance-authority</code> for the Button.
    </li>
    <li>
      Components use the standard shadcn tokens and your <code>cn()</code> helper, so installing one
      does not change your theme.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Every component imports <code>cn()</code> from shadcn's <code>utils</code> item. The current
    version of that item re-exports <code>cn()</code> from the <code>cn</code> npm package, so when
    the CLI installs or updates <code>lib/utils.ts</code>, it also adds <code>cn</code> to your
    dependencies. If your <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs, the CLI asks before replacing it;
    answering no is safe.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Optional theme</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The Buril palette, ink on paper with a red reserved for focus, decoration, the
    <code>--accent</code> hover wash and destructive actions, is a separate install:
  </p>
  <InstallCommand id="install-theme" commands={["add @buril/theme"]} />
  <p class="mt-4">It edits your global CSS file:</p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      It replaces your shadcn color tokens in <code>:root</code> and <code>.dark</code>. Other
      variables, such as <code>--radius</code>, stay as they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      It adds two decorative tokens, <code>--decor</code> and <code>--grid</code>, the font tokens
      <code>--font-display</code> and <code>--font-body</code>, and the 10 motion tokens:
      <code>--buril-duration-*</code> and <code>--buril-ease-*</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      It follows the system without JavaScript, and redefines the <code>dark:</code> variant the
      same way with <code>@custom-variant dark</code>. <Link href="/docs/theming">Theming</Link>
      explains dark mode and how to change the colors.
    </li>
    <li>
      It leaves the <code>--sidebar-*</code> and <code>--chart-*</code> tokens alone: Buril has no
      sidebar or chart components, so those keep your current values.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme is not the only item that edits your CSS: the <code>button</code> item appends the
    icon gesture keyframes, named <code>buril-icon-*</code>, which IconButton's
    <code>motion</code> prop plays.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Fonts</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme defines two font tokens, <code>--font-display</code> for headings and
    <code>--font-body</code> for text, both with system fallbacks. It does not apply them, and no
    component uses them, so set them where you want them in your global CSS:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={applyFontsExample} lang="css" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Buril ships no font files, so load your own fonts, then point the tokens at them, as
    <Link href="/docs/guides/theme">Customizing the theme</Link> shows. This site pairs Silkscreen,
    a pixel font kept for headings only, with Inter for everything else; both are free on Google
    Fonts.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Next steps</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <Link href="/docs/components">Browse the components</Link> and install the ones you need.
    </li>
    <li>
      <Link href="/docs/theming">Theming</Link> explains the tokens and dark mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <Link href="/docs/guides/forms">Forms with Field</Link> builds a complete form.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
